course: dictionary
lesson: 13768
title: Interstice: a tiny gap
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: An interstice is a very small gap or space between things that are close together. It is a formal word, so writers use it when they want to be precise. Pupils would often just say gap or space. «Water seeped into the interstices between the grains of sand.» The grains sit close together, and the tiny spaces between them are the interstices.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Between, grains and seeped into tell you that an interstice is a space that sits between things. «Moss grew in the interstices of the old stone wall.» Moss needs a place to grow, so the interstices must be small cracks in the wall. Try swapping in gap: the sentence still makes sense, so gap is a good meaning.
explain: Interstice can also mean a short gap in time, or a gap in a set of ideas. «There was a brief interstice in the storm, so we ran for home.» The storm paused for a moment, so the interstice is a short break. In everyday talk you can say gap, break or pause instead.
fact: An interstice is a very small gap or space between things that are close together.
fact: Interstice is a formal word; in everyday speech, people say gap or space.
fact: The plural is interstices, meaning several gaps, such as the spaces between bricks.
fact: Interstice can also mean a short gap in time or in a set of ideas.
fact: Phrases you may see include «the interstices of» and «in the interstices».
exercise: «Water filled the interstices between the grains of sand.» What is an interstice? | A wet, muddy path | A large heap of sand | *A very small gap between things | A kind of shell
exercise: «Moss grew in the interstices of the old wall.» Which everyday word could replace interstices? | *Gaps | Ladders | Songs | Colours
exercise: «There was a brief interstice in the storm.» What does interstice mean here? | A very large building | A type of fish | A loud shout | *A short pause in time
test: «Light came through the interstices of the leaves.» What are the interstices? | Loud birds in the branches | *Small gaps between the leaves | Bright colours on the leaves | The roots of the tree
test: Which everyday word is closest in meaning to interstice? | Hammer | Happy | *Gap | Mountain
exam: Why might a writer choose interstice instead of gap? | *Because it is a formal word that sounds precise | Because interstice means a big hole in the ground | Because interstice is shorter to write | Because gap is always wrong in writing
